Day 14 - PostgreSQL and Docker Compose

1. Project Overview

Today, I practiced setting up a PostgreSQL database using Docker Compose.

The objective was to understand how a database runs inside a container, how its data is stored safely, and how we can interact with it using SQL commands.

I also started preparing a Python application that will connect to PostgreSQL.

The main idea behind this practice was to understand how an application and a database work together in a real-world project.

2. Creating the Docker Compose Configuration

I created a directory named "day14-demo" and inside it created a file named "compose.yaml".

This file contains the instructions Docker Compose needs to run our database service.

Configuration

services:
  db:
    image: postgres:16-alpine
    environment:
      POSTGRES_USER: aman
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: YOUR_LOCAL_DEMO_PASSWORD
      POSTGRES_DB: notesdb
    volumes:
      - postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data

volumes:
  postgres_data:

Understanding the Configuration

services:

This section defines which services our project needs. A project can contain multiple services, such as a web application and a database.

db:

This is the name I assigned to the PostgreSQL service. Other services in the same Compose network can use this name to find the database.

image: postgres:16-alpine

This tells Docker to use the PostgreSQL version 16 Alpine image.

An image is a package containing the software and files needed to create a container.

environment:

Environment variables provide configuration settings to the container.

- "POSTGRES_USER": Defines the initial database username.
- "POSTGRES_PASSWORD": Sets the password for that database user.
- "POSTGRES_DB": Defines the initial database name, "notesdb".

The official PostgreSQL image uses these settings when initializing a new database data directory.

volumes:

A database needs persistent storage because its records should not disappear just because a container is removed.

I configured a named volume called "postgres_data".

"/var/lib/postgresql/data" is the directory inside the PostgreSQL container where its database files are stored.

The volume connects this directory to Docker-managed storage.

This allows the database data to survive container removal.

3. Validating the Configuration

Before starting PostgreSQL, I ran:

docker compose config

This command reads and displays the Compose configuration.

It helped me verify that Docker Compose could understand the YAML file without starting the container.

4. Starting the PostgreSQL Container

I started the database using:

sudo docker compose up -d

Understanding the command:

- "sudo": Runs the command with administrator permissions.
- "docker compose": Manages the services defined in "compose.yaml".
- "up": Creates and starts the required containers.
- "-d": Runs the containers in the background.

During this step, I encountered a Docker permission error.

The Linux user did not have permission to access the Docker daemon socket. I used "sudo" to execute the Docker commands successfully.

Docker downloaded the PostgreSQL image and started the database container.

5. Checking Container Status

I checked the container status using:

sudo docker compose ps

The PostgreSQL container showed an "Up" status.

This means the container was running.

I also learned that a running container and a database ready to accept connections are two different things. That is why checking the logs is important.

6. Checking PostgreSQL Logs

I ran:

sudo docker compose logs db

This command displays the logs generated by the PostgreSQL service.

I checked the startup messages and found:

"database system is ready to accept connections"

This confirmed that PostgreSQL had completed its startup process and was ready to receive database connections.

7. Connecting to PostgreSQL Using psql

Next, I accessed the database using:

sudo docker compose exec db psql -U aman -d notesdb

Understanding the command:

- "exec": Runs a command inside an already running container.
- "db": Selects the PostgreSQL service.
- "psql": PostgreSQL's command-line tool.
- "-U aman": Specifies the database username.
- "-d notesdb": Selects the database to open.

After running the command, I entered the PostgreSQL interface and saw the "notesdb=#" prompt.

This meant I could now execute SQL commands directly against the database.

8. Practicing Basic SQL Commands

SQL stands for Structured Query Language.

It is used to communicate with databases, create tables, insert records, and retrieve information.

Creating a Table

CREATE TABLE notes (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    note TEXT
);

I created a table named "notes".

Understanding the structure:

- "CREATE TABLE": Creates a new table.
- "notes": The name of the table.
- "id": A column used to identify each record.
- "SERIAL": Automatically generates an increasing number.
- "PRIMARY KEY": Ensures each record has a unique identifier.
- "note TEXT": Creates a column that stores text.

A table organizes information into rows and columns.

Inserting Data

INSERT INTO notes (note)
VALUES ('Learning Docker and PostgreSQL');

This command inserts a new record into the "notes" table.

- "INSERT INTO": Adds new data to a table.
- "notes": Specifies the table.
- "(note)": Specifies the column receiving the data.
- "VALUES": Specifies the actual value.

The database automatically generated the ID for this record.

Retrieving Data

SELECT * FROM notes;

This command retrieves all columns and records from the "notes" table.

I verified that the previously inserted note was successfully stored and displayed.

This helped me understand the basic database operations of creating a table, inserting information, and reading saved records.

Exiting PostgreSQL

\q

This exits the PostgreSQL command-line interface and returns to the normal Linux terminal.

It does not delete the database or its records.

9. Stopping and Removing Containers

After completing the database practice, I ran:

sudo docker compose down

This command stops and removes the containers and the Compose-created network.

An important point I learned is that this command does not remove named volumes by default.

Because I did not use the "-v" option, the "postgres_data" volume was preserved.

This is important for database applications because persistent data should remain available even when containers are recreated.

10. Preparing the Python Application

I also started preparing a Python application to connect with PostgreSQL.

I created a file named "app.py".

Inside the file, I added:

import psycopg

"import" is a Python statement used to make a library available in our code.

"psycopg" is a PostgreSQL database driver. A driver allows a Python application to communicate with PostgreSQL.

Checking Python

python3 --version

I verified that Python 3.14.4 was installed.

Creating a Virtual Environment

python3 -m venv .venv

A virtual environment creates a separate Python environment for a project.

This helps keep the project's installed libraries separate from other Python projects.

The required virtual environment support package was initially missing, so I installed it:

sudo apt install python3.14-venv

Then I successfully created the virtual environment.

Viewing Hidden Files

ls -a

I used this command to view the ".venv" directory.

On Linux, files and directories beginning with a dot are hidden by default, so normal "ls" does not display them.

Activating the Virtual Environment

source .venv/bin/activate

This activates the project's virtual environment.

After activation, the terminal displays "(.venv)".

The next planned step is to install the PostgreSQL Python driver and use it to connect the application to the database.

11. What I Understood Today

Through this practical exercise, I learned how different parts of a database-based application fit together:

1. Docker Compose defines and manages the database service.
2. The PostgreSQL image provides the database software.
3. Environment variables provide initial database settings.
4. Docker volumes preserve database files outside the container.
5. "psql" allows direct interaction with PostgreSQL.
6. SQL commands create tables, insert records, and retrieve data.
7. Python applications can communicate with PostgreSQL through a database driver.
8. Virtual environments keep Python project dependencies separate.


